<p>King Charles III joked at a White House state dinner on Tuesday that Americans would be speaking French without the British, responding to President Trump’s earlier claim that European countries would be speaking German without U.S. help in World War II. The king made the remark during dinner toasts with Trump, linking it to British and French colonial origins in North America before U.S. independence 250 years ago. The dinner was part of Charles’s U.S. state visit with Queen Camilla and followed a White House welcome in which Trump said the United States had no closer friends than the British. Some reports described strains over the Iran war; the dinner speeches emphasized the “special relationship” between London and Washington.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/RJki9CWBSGixaNwGfglnpWtFGEb9iqCFv0PLBdbgWyobVhtVHYji1y24BmE4JAacLMEPG4xjOJ5JagkTvvv_9bdgLttixMUAOu1-W0XMCZrv' alt='US President Donald Trump and Britain&#x27;s King Charles III smile as they talk during a State Dinner in the East Room of the White House in Washington, DC, on April 28, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>East Wing: Charles said he noticed “readjustments” to the White House East Wing, which Trump demolished to build a $400 million ballroom.</li><li>Tea reference: The king compared the state dinner favorably with the Boston Tea Party, when colonists dumped taxed British tea into the sea in 1773.</li><li>Moon joke: Charles joked about U.S. lunar plans, telling Trump he had checked the papers and suspected the moon was already part of the Commonwealth.</li></ul><blockquote>You recently commented, Mr President, that if it were not for the United States, European countries would be speaking German. Dare I say that, if it wasn’t for us, you’d be speaking French - King Charles III</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>King Charles III: Charles framed his remarks around British and French traces in U.S. place names, citing the Carolinas, Virginia, Georgia and Maryland before sayin